```c
sort_by_increasing_end(intervals);

int current_end = intervals[0].end;
int remove_count = 0;

for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
  if (intervals[i].start >= current_end) {
    // No overlap, move on to next interval
    current_end = intervals[i].end;
  } else {
    // Overlap! Remove current interval
    remove_count++;
  }
}
```

Idiom: Because intervals are sorted by end time, when two intervals overlap, the current interval ends no earlier than the last kept interval. Keeping the earlier-ending interval preserves the most room for future intervals.

Note: use `intervals[i].start > current_end` if touching counts as overlap.

